  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1202 PM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nWV imagery showing sub-tropical moisture plume over most of the CWA \nexcept the far west. Upper trough over eastern Arizona with strong \ndrying behind it. This trough will continue east today, reaching the \nRio Grande Valley later this afternoon. This likely will be the \nfocus for convection with scattered storms ahead of it and perhaps \njust a few to the west of the trough. PWs in the east today will \nstill remain around 1.1 to 1.2 inches, so some flash flooding \npossible again. A Flood Watch remains in effect for the Sacramento \nMtns through late tonight. The trough should be east of the area \nlate tonight as strong southwest flow continues around the main \nupper low over the Great Basin.\n\nSaturday through Monday...continued drying with a short break from \nmost thunderstorm activity. PWs drop to around .75 inches Saturday \nso maybe just a few thunderstorms over Otero/Hudspeth Counties. PWs \nfor Sunday/Monday drop even further and airmass lacks much of any \ninstability, so other than a slight chance of storms over the Sacs, \nthese two days should be dry.\n\nTuesday into the weekend...next Pacific low plows into SoCal Monday \nand lifts up over the Great Basin Tuesday. This will bring the sub-\ntropical jet back into play; by Tuesday the jet is over far western \nand northern New Mexico and by Wed/Thur extending SW-NE across \ncentral New Mexico. The main result of this is the jet bringing up \ncopious amounts of sub-tropicl moisture. Hence we are looking at \nanother stretch for a threat of heavy rain and flash flooding. PWs \nincrease back to the 1.3 to 1.5 inch range Tuesday through Friday \nfor the highest risk of flooding. Possible tropical storm remnants \ncould play into the scenario also. Both GFS/ECMWF begin thinning the \nmoisture plume by next Saturday and Sunday, but the chance of \nthunderstorms will continue.",
